;; Exemptions for `bb check:ports`.
;;
;; `:allow-cross-module-shell` is a BURN-DOWN LIST. Each entry names a target
;; prefix that shell namespaces in other modules currently reach into, and says
;; why that is tolerated for now.
;;
;; By prefix rather than by call site on purpose: 43 sites reach into 8 target
;; groups, and listing them one by one would hide that these are eight
;; decisions, not forty-three. Every entry MUST carry a `:why` — the gate throws
;; on one without it rather than honouring it (BOU-250).
;;
;; Composition roots — `*.shell.module-wiring`, `*.shell.system.wiring`,
;; `*.shell.cli-entry`, `*.shell.boot` — are exempt in code, not here. Wiring is
;; the one job that has to name concrete implementations.

{:allow-cross-module-shell
 [;; ==========================================================================
  ;; BOU-303 (M3) — platform has no database port
  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;; The largest group by far, and the one the ticket for this gate named. Every
  ;; module's persistence layer reaches straight into platform's JDBC adapters
  ;; because there is nothing else to reach for. A port closes all 20 at once.
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.platform.shell.adapters.database"
   :why "BOU-303. 20 sites across tenant, user, admin, workflow and devtools.
         platform exposes no database port, so a module's persistence layer has
         no sanctioned way to reach a connection. Clears when the port lands."}

  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;; Framework building blocks that live under shell/ but are not implementations
  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;; These are pipeline pieces every module composes into its own persistence and
  ;; service layers. Reaching for them is not reaching into platform's business
  ;; logic — platform has none. The honest fix is that they do not belong under
  ;; `shell/`, which is a rename, not a redesign.
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.platform.shell.persistence-interceptors"
   :why "BOU-307 follow-up. 6 sites. An interceptor pipeline every module's
         persistence composes; not an implementation of platform's own. Wrongly
         placed under shell/ rather than wrongly used — moving it is a rename
         with its own ticket."}
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.platform.shell.service-interceptors"
   :why "BOU-307 follow-up. 4 sites. Same shape as persistence-interceptors."}
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.platform.shell.interceptors"
   :why "BOU-307 follow-up. 1 site (user.shell.interceptors). Same shape."}

  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;; i18n — rendering infrastructure, used by every UI-bearing module
  ;; ==========================================================================
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.i18n.shell.render"
   :why "BOU-307 follow-up. 4 sites (admin, search, user, workflow). Resolving
         [:t :key] markers in a Hiccup tree is infrastructure every UI module
         needs, and i18n exposes it nowhere else. Needs a port or a non-shell
         home; the marker convention is ADR-013 and not in question."}
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.i18n.shell.middleware"
   :why "BOU-307 follow-up. 4 sites, same modules. The Ring middleware that puts
         the translation fn on the request. Same decision as render."}

  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;; Genuine cross-feature coupling — the ones that are actually wrong
  ;; ==========================================================================
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.user.shell.middleware"
   :why "BOU-307 follow-up. 2 sites (admin, workflow) reaching into the user
         module's auth middleware. This is a feature library depending on
         another feature library's shell — the shape this gate exists to catch.
         Needs an auth port, and is the first entry to burn down."}
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.platform.shell.web.table"
   :why "BOU-307 follow-up. 1 site (user.shell.web-handlers). A Hiccup table
         helper; same placement question as the interceptors."}

  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;; Bridging adapter — deliberate, and declared
  ;; ==========================================================================
  {:target-prefix "wagoe.external.shell.adapters.smtp"
   :why "email's SMTP adapter exists to bridge its Email domain model onto
         external's raw javax.mail transport, and constructing that transport
         means naming it. It talks to wagoe.external.ports for everything else
         and declares wagoe/external in deps.edn. Closing this properly means
         external exposing a constructor through its ports — worth doing, not
         urgent."}]}
